So your local setParam() should only return true when it has actually accepted a valid parameter-value pair. Returning true for a malformed value tells the helm, “this parameter was valid and configured,” even if the behavior now contains garbage or an unsafe value.

The two important fixes are:

Normalize the parameter name

C++
param = tolower(param);

MOOS-IvP behavior examples commonly convert behavior parameter names to lowercase so .bhv files are less fragile about capitalization. The official simple waypoint example does this before matching parameters. 
oceanai.mit.edu

Validate the value before accepting it

C++
isNumber(val)
radius > 0

atof() is dangerous by itself because invalid strings often become 0.0. So this:

C++
survey_radius = banana

could silently set m_radius to 0, and your behavior would still return true. In MOOS-IvP, that is bad because returning true suppresses the normal “unhandled/bad parameter” failure path. The behavior should reject bad config so the helm/appcasting warnings expose the problem.

The pattern is:

C++
accept known + valid parameter  -> set member variable, return true
unknown or invalid parameter    -> return false

For a custom behavior, that is the difference between a bad .bhv file being visible during startup versus a mission that “runs” with a quietly broken behavior.

