The bug is that setParam() never calls the parent class's setParam(). Here's the fix:

cpp
bool BHV_Survey::setParam(string param, string val)
{
  if(param == "survey_radius") {
    m_radius = atof(val.c_str());
    return true;
  }
  return IvPBehavior::setParam(param, val);  // ← delegate to parent
}

Why this matters in MOOS-IvP:

IvPBehavior::setParam() is the base class that handles all the standard behavior parameters — things like pwt, duration, condition, updates, endflag, etc. These aren't magic; they're parsed by the base class's own setParam() implementation.

When your override returns false for any unrecognized param (as the original code does), the behavior framework interprets that as a configuration error and will typically refuse to load the behavior entirely — even if the unknown param was something completely legitimate like pwt = 100.

The correct pattern in MOOS-IvP behavior development is:

Handle your custom params first.
Fall through to IvPBehavior::setParam(param, val) for everything else, and return whatever it returns.

This way, standard params get processed correctly, unknown params still return false (propagated from the base), and your behavior loads cleanly.

