You are spot on—that implementation is indeed incorrect for a MOOS-IvP behavior.

While the logic looks like standard C++, it breaks the initialization protocol of the IvPDomain and IvPBehavior architecture.

Here is the corrected version, followed by the specific reasons why your original code would cause issues.

C++
bool BHV_Survey::setParam(string param, string val)
{
  // First, pass the parameter up to the base class handler
  if(IvPBehavior::setParam(param, val))
    return(true);

  if(param == "survey_radius") {
    bool handled = setDoubleOnString(m_radius, val);
    return(handled);
  }

  return(false);
}

The MOOS-IvP-Specific Reasons
1. Breaking the Base Class Chain (IvPBehavior::setParam)

In MOOS-IvP, your custom behavior (BHV_Survey) inherits from IvPBehavior. The base class handles a massive amount of critical configuration parameters that apply to all behaviors (e.g., pwt for priority weight, condition, updates, endflag, duration).

By returning false for anything that isn't "survey_radius", your original code completely blocks the base class from ever seeing or processing its own parameters. If a user tried to set condition = DEPLOY=true in the .bhv file for your behavior, the helm would throw a configuration error and crash on startup.

2. Lack of Type and Syntax Safety (setDoubleOnString)

Using standard atof(val.c_str()) is risky in a robust robotic architecture. If a user accidentally types a typo in the .bhv file (like survey_radius = ten), atof will silently return 0.0 without warning, and your behavior will run with a broken radius.

MOOS-IvP provides native utility functions like setDoubleOnString(), setBooleanOnString(), and setUIntOnString(). These functions do two crucial things:

They attempt to parse the string safely.

They return a bool (true if parsing succeeded, false if it failed).

By returning handled, you ensure that if someone inputs garbage data, the Helm will catch it during the configuration phase, output a helpful error message to the terminal, and prevent the vehicle from launching with bad parameters.

